## Traversal and Movement

- Web swinging has been completely rewritten with more physical, rope-like movement.
- Web ropes now behave more naturally while swinging, pulling, and changing direction.
- Previously fired webs can remain attached to the environment instead of immediately disappearing.
- Players can have up to eight standard webs active in the world.
- Swinging now has a velocity cap to keep traversal controllable at extreme speeds.
- Swing assist has been reworked to support the new swinging physics.
- Web zipping now uses a stamina and recharge system.
- Zip strength and maximum zip speed have been increased.
- Perch Zipping allows players to accurately zip directly to targeted ledges and surfaces.
- Players can zip directly to Web Bridges and portals.
- Physzipping allows players to launch themselves while tethered to the same object or enemy with both hands.
- Movement now begins at a walking pace and accelerates naturally into a run.
- Stopping gradually returns the player to walking speed.
- Being airborne or tethered immediately transitions the player to running speed.
- Sprinting provides an additional burst of speed beyond the normal running pace.
- Desktop players can sprint with Shift when double-tap flipping is enabled.
- VR players can sprint through increased hand movement.
- Landing at high velocity carries momentum into ground movement.
- High-speed landings can create a temporary slide before returning to regular movement.
- Flipping now follows the player’s head rotation for consistent controls at any body orientation.
- Flipping can automatically align the player with the trajectory of their webs.
- A separate Rotate on Web Grab option allows automatic rotation to be limited to Web Grabbing.
- Full-body feet tracking is supported for combat.
- Avatar size now affects web thickness, menu positioning, flip collision size, movement input scaling, and footstep pitch.

