## Human Enemies and Combat AI

- Fully voiced human enemies have been added.
- Enemy archetypes include melee fighters, bludgeoners, handgun users, riot units, snipers, SMG units, rocketeers, drone operators, and specialized support enemies.
- Enemies use hazard detection around rooftops and ledges.
- Enemy accuracy, damage, and perception scale with combat difficulty.
- Enemies use distinct damage values and firing intervals based on their weapon type.
- Snipers patrol normally but maintain distance rather than directly chasing the player.
- Enemies equipped with two-handed weapons can throw grenades, except for rocketeers.
- Enemy grenades can be grabbed, webbed, and thrown back.
- Rockets can be caught with webs or hands and redirected using the opposite hand’s crosshair.
- Enemies can deploy explosive tripwires.
- Snipers, operators, and zombies can deploy grapple hooks.
- Enemy grapple hooks can attach to walls, objects, drones, and players.
- A grappled player can be shocked and damaged.
- SMG and rocket enemies can deploy parachutes.
- Parachuting rocket enemies can continue firing while airborne.
- Players can swing from parachuting enemies without immediately pulling them down.
- Using both hands allows the player to take control of a parachuting enemy’s movement.
- Enemies can suffer lethal long falls.
- Wrapped or hanging enemies are exempt from normal terminal-velocity calculations.
- High-velocity enemy impacts create dedicated crash effects.
- Enemies use over two dozen contextual taunts and combat voice lines.
- Additional reactions play when enemies are tethered, collide with one another, parachute, grapple, or are physically held.
- A new Sniper Tower enemy can be pulled down with webs or destroyed using explosives and thrown objects.
- Web yanking an enemy from below can slam them directly into the ground.


